  "brief_md": "# O03 — Automated ADA/WCAG Accessibility Audit Platform for Municipalities\n\n## Payer\nMunicipal governments — specifically the IT departments, city clerks, and ADA coordinators at the ~17,000+ US municipalities under 50,000 population now facing an April 26, 2028 compliance deadline (extended from 2027), and larger jurisdictions (50,000+) facing an April 26, 2027 deadline (extended from April 2026) ([ADA.gov](https://www.ada.gov/resources/web-rule-first-steps/), [Federal Register IFR](https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/04/20/2026-07663/extension-of-compliance-dates-for-nondiscrimination-on-the-basis-of-disability-accessibility-of-web)). Price anchor: GovAccess sells a WCAG scanner + compliance checklist at **$149/month** ([govaccess.us](https://www.govaccess.us/)); CASO Comply prices automated PDF remediation at **$0.30/page**, i.e., ~$900 for a 3,000-page municipal site ([casocomply.com](https://casocomply.com/solutions/municipal)).\n\n## Competitors\n- **GovAccess** — $149/month scanner + free 2-minute audit lead magnet ([govaccess.us](https://www.govaccess.us/))\n- **Quillinox** — unpublished pricing; demo-gated; positions itself as the full \"system of record\" with cryptographic audit trail and six-document legal defense package ([quillinox.com](https://quillinox.com/))\n- **CASO Comply** — $0.30/page standard, $1.80/page \"enhanced\" with 95%+ guarantee, free SiteScan ([casocomply.com](https://casocomply.com/solutions/municipal))\n- **CivicPlus / AudioEye** — e.g., $2,510 quoted to the Town of Templeton, MA, vs. $24,281 for a full redesign ([Citizen Portal](https://citizenportal.ai/articles/6372569/Massachusetts/Worcester-County/Town-of-Templeton/Town-hears-CivicPlus-pitch-to-upgrade-website-add-AudioEye-accessibility-tools)); CivicPlus itself serves 10,000+ government customers ([AudioEye](https://www.audioeye.com/post/audioeye-and-civicplus-partner-for-improved-government-accessibility-and-compliance/))\n- **Continual Engine** — Cole County, MO paid $7,000 for an AI-powered grading/compliance tool in March 2026 ([Jefferson City News Tribune](https://www.newstribune.com/news/2026/mar/29/ada-compliance-deadline-looms-county-opinions/))\n- Adjacent: accessiBe (widget-based, not government-specific)\n\n## Acquisition Channels\n1. **Municipal league conference exhibit booths** (NLC Congress of Cities/City Summit, state leagues): historically $1,900–$2,600 per 10'x10' booth ([2015 NLC prospectus via silo.tips](https://silo.tips/download/exhibitor-prospectus-join-city-leaders-from-across-the-country-in)); state-league booths (e.g., League of MN Cities 2026) are already sold out, implying competition for this channel ([lmc.org](https://www.lmc.org/learning-events/events/league-events/2026-lmc-annual-conference/information-for-exhibitors/)).\n2. **Free audit / SiteScan as inbound lead magnet** — the exact funnel GovAccess and CASO Comply already run; marketing-automation tooling runs roughly $10–$209/month ([PhantomBuster](https://phantombuster.com/blog/outbound-sales/lead-generation-cost/)).\n\n## Demand Evidence\n- Eric Landwehr, Cole County (MO) Public Works director: *\"We're having to adhere to this federal mandate, and the way to do it is just remove information... I don't think that's right. It's counterproductive.\"* ([News Tribune](https://www.newstribune.com/news/2026/mar/29/ada-compliance-deadline-looms-county-opinions/))\n- Larry Vincent, Cole County Collector: *\"None of my people are IT people... taking on new projects that are hit and miss because we're not familiar with it.\"* ([News Tribune](https://www.newstribune.com/news/2026/mar/29/ada-compliance-deadline-looms-county-opinions/))\n\n## Case For\nThe core technical stack (axe-core scanning, AI-based PDF/UA tagging) is already commercially proven at commodity prices, so an AI-agent-run solo shop could replicate the scan + prioritized-fix-list layer cheaply and ride the same free-audit lead-generation funnel competitors use. Pain is concrete, dated, and documented: small-county IT departments of 1-4 people have already spent real money ($7,000+) under deadline pressure, and sales copy from multiple incumbents independently confirms \"no dedicated accessibility staff\" is the norm for sub-50,000 municipalities.\n\n## Case Against\nAt least five direct competitors already occupy this space with deeper government-specific moats (cryptographic audit trails, VPAT generation, SOC2/HIPAA-ready infrastructure, 40-year government relationships, 10,000+ existing CivicPlus customers). The commodity scanning/remediation layer is already priced near-zero margin ($0.30/page), while the layer with real pricing power — legally defensible compliance documentation — carries liability risk a solo, uninsured operator is poorly positioned to absorb if a municipality relies on faulty compliance claims in a DOJ complaint or lawsuit. Municipal procurement (RFPs, insurance certificates, cooperative-purchasing vetting) is slow and bureaucratic, a poor fit for a fast six-week build-and-sell cycle. The DOJ has already pushed this deadline back once, and the urgency the whole pitch depends on could erode further with additional delays or lax enforcement.\n\n## Open Questions\n- Does a six-week MVP (scanner-only) reach a defensible value proposition, or does the market actually require the costlier legal-defense-package layer incumbents provide?\n- What is the real procurement timeline and insurance/reference burden for a solo vendor selling directly to municipalities?\n- How many under-50,000-population municipalities remain unserved by incumbents and have budget before the 2028 deadline?\n- What E&O/liability exposure exists if the tool's compliance claims are later contested, and can a one-person company obtain affordable coverage?\n- Will DOJ enforcement materialize meaningfully, or will further extensions/lax enforcement reduce willingness to pay?"
